# AWS-0104 — "A security group rule should not allow unrestricted egress to
# any IP address." (aka AVD-AWS-0104, no-public-egress-sgr)
#
# Not our code. The finding is the `egress_all` rule at node_groups.tf:175 of
# terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws, which Trivy reaches because a config scan
# downloads remote modules even though this job never runs `terraform init`.
#
# Why it is not simply switched off: that rule sits inside
# local.node_security_group_recommended_rules, gated by the module's
# node_security_group_enable_recommended_rules (default true). The same flag
# also controls the cluster-to-node webhook ingress on 4443/6443/8443/9443 that
# metrics-server, prometheus-adapter, the ALB controller and Karpenter rely on.
# The ungated rule set next to it is ingress-only, so disabling the flag leaves
# the node security group with no egress at all and nodes cannot reach the EKS
# API or pull from ECR.
#
# Why a narrower rule would not clear it either: the check keys on the /0
# destination, not the port range. Verified against Trivy directly — an egress
# rule scoped to tcp/443 with cidr 0.0.0.0/0 still fires; the same rule scoped
# to 10.0.0.0/16 does not.
#
# The real fix is interface endpoints for ECR API, ECR DKR, STS, EKS and
# CloudWatch Logs plus an S3 gateway endpoint, then egress restricted to the VPC
# CIDR and prefix lists. That is a deliberate piece of work with a standing
# monthly cost, not a cleanup, so this is an accepted exception until then.
#
# Re-check when the eks module is next upgraded: if egress_all becomes
# independently configurable, delete this entry.
AVD-AWS-0104

# AWS-0040 — "Public cluster access is enabled."
# AWS-0041 — "Cluster allows access from a public CIDR."
#
# These two are our own choice, not an upstream default: eks.tf sets
# cluster_endpoint_public_access = true so the cluster can be driven with
# kubectl and helm from a workstation during a demo.
#
# Neither is clearable by configuration. Verified against Trivy directly:
# with cluster_endpoint_public_access_cidrs narrowed to a single /32, AWS-0041
# still fires and only changes its message to name that /32, because the check
# flags any public CIDR rather than a wide one. AWS-0040 fires on the public
# endpoint existing at all. The only configuration that satisfies both is a
# private-only endpoint.
#
# What was actually done about the risk: the access list is now
# var.cluster_public_access_cidrs instead of the module default of 0.0.0.0/0,
# so a run can be scoped to one address without editing Terraform. The default
# is still 0.0.0.0/0 for demo convenience, so on a default apply this is a knob
# rather than a mitigation. The endpoint requires IAM auth in every case.
#
# The real fix is a private endpoint reached over SSM port-forwarding, which
# clears both. Worth doing if this cluster ever outlives a demo.
AVD-AWS-0040
AVD-AWS-0041
